 "customerTurns": 4,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Our Bosch dryer keeps stopping with the empty water container light on but the container is empty?? We've emptied it like 5 times"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is reseat the water container and check its detection; confirm condensate is actually being produced. Can you tell me whether container not fully home?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes, I've pushed the container in firmly and it clicks into place. Still does it though. It's a Bosch, the manual says WTG8-something."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is float / level switch. Behind the flap at the bottom front there's a condenser unit (or a second filter on heat-pump models). Take it out and rinse the fluff off under the tap, then let it drip-dry and refit it. Was it clogged with fluff (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the condenser and water tank are\" — On a condenser dryer the water container pulls out at the top and the condenser/heat exchanger sits behind a flap at the bottom front — slide it out and rinse it. Condenser/heat-pump dryers only; layout may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I cleaned the condenser and the fluff filters too, they were fairly clean already. Still stops with the light on though."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: float switch]\nThe checks point to the float switch. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on a sticker inside the door opening.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I only know it starts WTG8 from the manual cover. The full label is on the back and the dryer is stacked on the washer so I can't get to it or move it. Is there anything else I can check from the front?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: float switch]\nYes, you should check if the water container sensor or its contacts are dirty and wipe them clean. You can also try resetting the appliance by unplugging it for a few minutes before plugging it back in. The checks point to the float switch. I could not find a confirmed match for model WTG8 in our parts catalogue, so check with the manufacturer or a parts supplier using the full model number, or ask an appliance engineer to fit it."
  }
 ],
